---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import LevelCalculator from '../../components/LevelCalculator.tsx';

const title = 'SPL & amplifier';
const description =
  'Home cinema SPL and amplifier calculator: can your speakers and amplifier reach Dolby’s calibration level? Power needed, maximum level and headroom for every speaker at the main seat.';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How much amplifier power does a home cinema need?',
    a: 'It depends on speaker sensitivity and the distance to the seat. Every 3 dB more needs twice the power, and every doubling of distance costs 6 dB in the free field. The calculator shows the power each speaker needs for full scale at the main seat.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does 2.83 V / 1 m mean for sensitivity?',
    a: 'Many manufacturers measure sensitivity with 2.83 volts rather than 1 watt. Into 8 ohms that is about 1 W, into 4 ohms about 2 W – so a 4-ohm speaker looks 3 dB more sensitive than it would at 1 W. The calculator accounts for that.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What level should I aim for?',
    a: 'Dolby’s home theater guidelines call for a calibration level of 79 to 82 dB SPL (C-weighted) at the main seat with pink noise at −20 dBFS, depending on room size. At full scale the level is 20 dB higher.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: 'Plan My Cinema – SPL & amplifier',
    url: 'https://plan-my-cinema.com/en/spl-amplifier/',
    applicationCategory: 'UtilitiesApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Web',
    inLanguage: 'en',
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'EUR' },
    description,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
  },
];
---
<Base lang="en" page="levels" title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>SPL <span>&amp;</span> amplifier</h1>
    <p>Can your speakers and amplifier reach the level your cinema is calibrated to? Layout, positions and seat come from the speaker planner.</p>
  </div>

  <LevelCalculator client:load lang="en" />

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <h3>Target level</h3>
    <p>
      Dolby’s <a href="https://www.dolby.com/siteassets/technologies/dolby-atmos/atmos-installation-guidelines-121318_r3.1.pdf" rel="noopener">Dolby Atmos Home Theater Installation Guidelines</a>{' '}
      (December 2018, p. 15) recommend calibrating every speaker to 79–82 dB SPL (C-weighted) at the main seat, depending on room size, using pink noise
      at −20 dBFS RMS. At full scale (0 dBFS) the level is 20 dB higher – 102 dB for an 82 dB calibration. Each speaker should be able to reach that at
      the main seat.
    </p>
    <h3>Level and power</h3>
    <p><code>Level = sensitivity + 10 · log10(P / P_ref) − 20 · log10(distance / 1 m)</code></p>
    <p>
      P_ref is 1 W for a 1 W / 1 m rating and 2.83² / impedance for a 2.83 V / 1 m rating. Twice the power adds 3 dB; twice the distance costs 6 dB.
      From this the calculator derives the power needed for the target and the maximum level your amplifier delivers, using distances from the speaker
      planner.
    </p>
    <h3>Limits</h3>
    <p>
      The calculation assumes a free field without reflections from walls and ceiling – real rooms are usually a little louder, so the result errs on
      the safe side. It ignores speaker power handling, power compression at high levels, several channels playing together, and subwoofers and the LFE
      channel. Calling less than 3 dB of headroom “tight” is our own choice.
    </p>

    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    {faq.map((f) => (
      <details>
        <summary>{f.q}</summary>
        <p>{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))}
  </article>
</Base>
